  "tour": {
    "title": "Hesed: Loyal Love in Action Across the Old Testament",
    "intro": "Start with the translation problem, then listen for the common thread across different contexts. The teachers use “loyal love,” “goodness in action,” and “covenant faithfulness,” not because hesed means anything at all, but because one English word cannot hold all its relational, committed, generous action. The tour moves from God’s self-description in Exodus 34 and the sweeping refrain of Psalm 136 to Ruth and 2 Samuel, where hesed is embodied by people.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:loyal-love-god",
        "startSec": 650,
        "endSec": 948,
        "why": "Begin with the word itself and the translation question. The speakers say translators are “struggling to find an easy one-for-one correspondence,” then describe hesed as love shown while “keeping a promise,” going “above and beyond,” and combining “generosity, commitment, and affection all in one.” This gives you a semantic center without pretending that “mercy,” “kindness,” “steadfast love,” or “loyal love” is a complete equivalent.",
        "episodeTitle": "The Loyal Love of God",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f",
        "durationSec": 4105,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:c416445833e65b5f726afb158ec92353",
        "startSec": 1400,
        "endSec": 1651,
        "why": "Now hear the word in Yahweh’s own self-description in Exodus 34:6–7. The episode identifies “loving kindness” as chesed and uses Luther’s phrase “goodness in action,” stressing that this is not merely an inward emotion but love moving outward in concrete forgiveness—while the same passage still addresses guilt and punishment. This establishes the divine portrait against which the later human examples can be heard.",
        "episodeTitle": "Episode 105 (Exodus 33:18-34:16)",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_Min_ep_105.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2488,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:a07922e45da2445b8c36defa6d82cc67",
        "startSec": 1474,
        "endSec": 1750,
        "why": "Psalm 136 widens the lens. The teachers follow the repeated refrain, “for his chesed, his steadfast love, his covenant faithfulness, however you want to translate that, loving kindness is everlasting,” through creation and the Exodus. They call the refrain an “interpretive key to the Torah,” showing hesed as the stated ground of God’s cosmic work, historical rescue, provision, and judgment.",
        "episodeTitle": "Live From HWSS 2019",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_Min_HWSS.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2510,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:a88b1fb7-fd1c-415f-b24c-98322c767992",
        "startSec": 870,
        "endSec": 1125,
        "why": "Ruth shows that hesed is not only a word for God’s character. Naomi asks Yahweh to “deal kindly” with her daughters-in-law, and the speakers identify that phrase as hesed, preferring “covenant faithfulness” while also discussing loyalty and steadfast love. Ruth’s decision to stay—“Your people shall be my people, and your God my God”—is then described as Hesed: costly, relational loyalty that crosses national and religious boundaries.",
        "episodeTitle": "Ruth 1-2: God Fills What's Empty",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG9761838334.mp3?updated=1664486106",
        "durationSec": 3645,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:478e6896-2d24-4806-ac5f-4db234766229",
        "startSec": 1840,
        "endSec": 2050,
        "why": "Finish with David’s treatment of Mephibosheth in 2 Samuel 9, where the abstract definition becomes startlingly concrete. David says, “I will show you chesed for the sake of your father Jonathan,” and expresses it by restoring Saul’s land and giving Mephibosheth a permanent place at the king’s table. The episode highlights this as an “unexpected gift,” showing the generosity and covenant memory that distinguish hesed from mere sentiment or bare obligation.",
        "episodeTitle": "2 Samuel 8:9-9:13 (Episode 267)",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_Min_ep_667.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2471,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips give you a usable answer: hesed is committed love or loyalty that acts within an enduring relationship—keeping faith, showing generosity, and often going beyond what is minimally required. Its range is the point: God’s character in Exodus, the reason given for creation and redemption in Psalm 136, Ruth’s costly allegiance, and David’s table fellowship. When you meet hesed again, ask what relationship is being honored and what concrete goodness the word produces; then let the context decide whether “mercy,” “kindness,” “steadfast love,” or another gloss best brings that facet across.",
    "totalSeconds": 1290
  },
